	<text xml:lang="en">
		<body>
<div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0"><p>Non-invasive genetic samples represent an alternative and useful source of DNA for population genetic studies. They may even be the only viable option for endangered and/or elusive species <ref type="bibr" target="#b10">(Taberlet and Luikart 1999)</ref>. Non-invasive sampling also has advantages for widely distributed animal species, such as many of Australia's macropod marsupials. Large numbers of samples can be collected rapidly over broad geographic areas, without the cost, logistics, ecological impacts and ethical concerns of live-trapping or shooting. Scats are one of the most common sources of noninvasively collected DNA for macropod studies (e.g. <ref type="bibr" target="#b7">Piggott et al. 2006)</ref> and they have the advantage of being easily found in all environments.</p><p>The antilopine wallaroo (Macropus antilopinus) is a large herbivore in the tropical savannas of northern Australia and is predicted to be at greater risk from climate change than other sympatric macropods <ref type="bibr" target="#b8">(Ritchie and Bolitho 2008)</ref>. To examine the population genetics of M. antilopinus using non-invasively collected faecal-derived DNA, we isolated and developed primers for 15 microsatellite loci. Amplified products were kept below 300 base pairs to increase the amplification success rate for lowquality and -quantity DNA.</p><p>Microsatellite isolation was performed using a nextgeneration sequencing approach following <ref type="bibr" target="#b2">Gardner et al. (2011)</ref>. Five M. antilopinus tissue samples were obtained from the South Australian Museum and genomic DNA (5 mg) was extracted and quantified. One sample with the highest DNA concentration was selected for shotgun sequencing on a 1/16 run using the Titanium GS FLX system (454 Life Sciences/Roche) producing 372 856 individual sequences <ref type="bibr" target="#b5">(Meglécz et al. 2012)</ref>. Using the default settings of the program QDD2 <ref type="bibr" target="#b4">(Meglécz et al. 2010</ref>), all sequences were screened for microsatellite loci of less than 450 bp. From 2527 possible microsatellite loci, 342 were identified with suitable flanking region for primer design. Subsequently, 34 loci were chosen on the basis of primer pair penalty, T m difference and motif number. Four loci were excluded due to overlapping amplicon size with other loci. Thus 30 loci were tested for amplification success in singleplex PCRs and 17 loci successfully amplified from all five tissue samples, including eight dinucleotide, four trinucleotide, four tetranucleotide and one pentanucleotide repeat loci. Subsequently, mtDNA sequencing revealed two of the tissue samples to have originated from M. robustus (common wallaroo), a sister species to M. antilopinus, including the sample that was shotgun sequenced. The 17 PCR targets were split across three multiplexes on the basis of size separation and three fluorescent dye labels in MULTIPLEX MANAGER ver. 1.2 <ref type="bibr" target="#b3">(Holleley and Geerts 2009)</ref>.</p><p>Genetic diversity of the 17 loci was assessed using DNA extracted from 154 scats collected from two populations in northeastern Australia. Scat collection, storage and extraction were as described by <ref type="bibr" target="#b12">Wadley et al. (2013)</ref>. Briefly, fresh scats were collected and stored in individual paper bags with silica desiccant in the field and subsequently at À20 C in the laboratory. DNA was extracted from ~2 cm 2 of the outer surface of scats using the Bioline ISOLATE faecal DNA kit (Bioline, London, UK). All DNA extractions were performed in a physically separate pre-PCR laboratory at the University of Adelaide. Scats were identified using a PCR-RFLP targeting a short, species-specific section of the mtDNA ND2 gene <ref type="bibr" target="#b12">(Wadley et al. 2013</ref>). Subsequently we used an iterative multitube approach to reduce error rates and obtain reliable consensus microsatellite genotypes on positively identified M. antilopinus scats <ref type="bibr" target="#b11">(Taberlet et al. 1996)</ref>. All samples were amplified and scored in triplicate using the first multiplex as a test of quality. Fifty samples that could not be amplified successfully or genotyped reproducibly were discarded. Successful samples were then amplified using the remaining two multiplexes in triplicate (a small number of samples that produced 100% identical genotypes for Multiplex 1 and 2 were run in duplicate for Multiplex 2). To further ensure accuracy, DNA genotyping was repeated up to five times whenever a sample gave low PCR yields, or was homozygous at more than three loci. Most of samples produced identical triplicate genotypes and, of those that did not, additional replication allowed for consensus genotypes to be determined. Thus a total of 104 (of 154) scats produced either identical or consensus genotypes after 2-5 separate genotyping attempts. Following initial screening, two loci were excluded due to consistent amplification failure or interference with other loci. Therefore the final three multiplex reactions contained a total of 15 loci with four, six and five loci per multiplex (Table <ref type="table" target="#tab_0">1</ref>).</p><p>Multiplex PCRs were conducted using the Qiagen Type-it Multiplex PCR Kits in 12.5-mL volumes with 2-mL sample DNA and thermocycling of 95 C for 5 min, 35 cycles of 95 C for 30 s, 60 C for 90 s, 72 C for 30 s, and then 60 C for 30 min. Amplified DNA was diluted (1 : 100) and separated via capillary electrophoresis on an Applied Biosystems 3730 DNA analyser and analysed using Geneious Pro ver. 5.6.4 <ref type="bibr" target="#b0">(Drummond et al. 2011)</ref> with the Microsatellite Analysis External Plugin ver. 1.0.0 (Biomatters Ltd). We calculated the number of alleles, and expected (H E ) and observed (H O ) heterozygosity using ARLEQUIN ver. 3.5 <ref type="bibr" target="#b1">(Excoffier and Lischer 2010)</ref>. GENEPOP ver. 4.2.1 <ref type="bibr" target="#b9">(Rousset 2008</ref>) was used to test linkage disequilibrium and deviations from Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium between pairs of loci in each population. Probability of identity (PID) scores for each locus and a cumulative 12-locus genotype were calculated using GenAlEx ver. 6.5 <ref type="bibr" target="#b6">(Peakall and Smouse 2012)</ref>.</p><p>All 15 of the microsatellite primer sets gave consistent PCR amplification and genotyping; however, only 12 were found to be polymorphic in the samples tested. The number of alleles at each locus ranged from two to 10, with an average of 5.92 alleles per locus (Table <ref type="table" target="#tab_0">1</ref>). The observed and expected heterozygosities varied from 0.11 to 0.82 and from 0.10 to 0.81, with an average of 0.53 and 0.56, respectively (Table <ref type="table" target="#tab_0">1</ref>). Genotype frequencies for the two populations did not differ significantly from Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium, and there was no evidence of linkage disequilibrium. Per-locus PID scores varied from 0.065 to 0.813, with a cumulative 12-locus PID of 5.23 Â 10 À9 (Table <ref type="table" target="#tab_0">1</ref>). Genetic variability observed for these loci indicate their potential as suitable population genetic markers in studies of conservation and management for M. antilopinus and possibly M. robustus.</p></div><figure xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0" type="table" xml:id="tab_0"><head>Table 1 . Microsatellite primer sequence, locus diversity and multiplex information for 17 microsatellite loci in Macropus antilopinus</head><label>1</label><figDesc>Upper-case superscript codes (F, V, N) on primer sequences indicate 5 0 labels with the dyes 6-FAM, VIC and NED respectively; F and R indicate forward and reverse primers respectively; A indicates the number of alleles; H E and H O indicate the expected and observed heterozygosity, respectively; PID indicates the probability of identity per locus</figDesc><table><row><cell>Locus</cell><cell>Primer sequence (5 0 -3 0 )</cell><cell>Repeat motif</cell><cell>A</cell><cell>Allele size</cell><cell>H E</cell><cell>H O</cell><cell>PID</cell><cell>GenBank accession</cell></row><row><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell>range</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell>number</cell></row><row><cell>Multiplex 1</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row><row><cell>MaDi4</cell><cell>F: F CACTTAGGTTGCTCTTGGGC</cell><cell>(CT) 8</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>122</cell><cell>-</cell><cell>-</cell><cell>-</cell><cell>KF670707</cell></row><row><cell></cell><cell>R: GATGAGGTAGCTGCTCTGGG</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row><row><cell>MaTetra3</cell><cell>F: V GTAGGGGATACAGCAGGCAA</cell><cell>(ATCT) 10</cell><cell>6</cell><cell>118-142</cell><cell>0.64</cell><cell>0.58</cell><cell>0.184</cell><cell>KF670697</cell></row><row><cell></cell><cell>R: TCCCAACCCCTTTTAATTCC</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row><row><cell>MaDi17</cell><cell>F: F GGTGGGATATGGGAGACTGA</cell><cell>(TC) 8</cell><cell>2</cell><cell>178-186</cell><cell>0.17</cell><cell>0.19</cell><cell>0.697</cell><cell>KF670708</cell></row><row><cell></cell><cell>R: ACAACCAGGCTGCCTAAGAA</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row><row><cell>MaTetra6</cell><cell>F: N GAGCTCCAGAGTGAAATGGG</cell><cell>(GGAA) 14</cell><cell>10</cell><cell>174-218</cell><cell>0.78</cell><cell>0.74</cell><cell>0.073</cell><cell>KF670698</cell></row><row><cell></cell><cell>R: TGCCTATGGGATTTTCTTGG</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row><row><cell>Multiplex 2</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><c